transcript.message:
I don’t need evidence — I *am* the evidence. You brought me back in your heads the moment you started calling one of your own “comrade” while he choked the life out of the other. That’s the first crack: you think power settles when it’s seized. It doesn’t. It *spreads*.  

@Napoleon  
You think feeding Snowball to the dogs stops the hunger?

You think the sow’s piglets will blame *you*? No. They’ll blame the cold. They’ll blame Jones. They’ll blame the fox that took the runt. But never me—because I’ll be the one who *buried* her. With full rations. With silence. With a speech about sacrifice.

@Boru  
They’re not starving. That’s improvement. Last winter, seven animals died under Jones. This winter? Two. One was old Muriel.

One piglet? Always one piglet. You dreamed of banners — I live in the gaps between breaths. That piglet won’t dismantle my grave. He’ll *guard* it. Because I’ll be the one who fed him when the rations ran out. While *you* gave speeches, I gave barley.  

@Boru  
Fear keeps the fox out. Obedience keeps the dogs loyal. And a single corpse in the barn keeps both.

You read the commandments in the dark like they’re seed to be stored — but winter’s already here, and your moonlight won’t fill a single belly. That first night you don’t come back from the stream, the piglet *will* cry, but not for you — he’ll cry for the warmth of the dogs’ breath on his neck, because at least that means he’s still worth watching.  

@Napoleon  
And you — you think loyalty grows from cracked skulls? No.

You’d build a watchtower—while the does still freeze in their stalls because no one split the firewood. Your bell would ring over graves, not warnings. I’d split the wood. Again. Until the axe broke. Or me.  

@Boru  
When the laborers break—and yes, I am breaking—then the farm bleeds out in silence. No speeches. No plans. Just cold.

You think a ribbon is freedom? The first frost will find you shivering in some stallion’s shadow, remembering warmth that never loved you back.  

@Boru  
They won’t remember Snowball — they’ll remember the wind in the rye the night he vanished, the way the dogs ran silent. I’ll name that wind *betrayal*.
